# C++ Coding Standards

## Class Design
1. Every class that is not inherited today - make final.
2. All headers that are redundant remove.
3. All methods that can be static make static.
4. All methods that cannot be made static but can be made const, make const.

## Code Quality
- Follow modern C++ best practices
- Use RAII principles
- Prefer smart pointers over raw pointers
- Use const correctness where possible
- Minimize header dependencies

## Header Management
- When a type is referenced as pointer or reference in a header and is part of the private definition of the class, use forward declaration
- If a type is part of the public API (method parameters, return types, public members), include the header that defines the type
- Only include headers for types that are actually used in the header file (not just declared)

## Header Include Organization
1. Local project headers (headers from the same project/directory)
2. Solution headers (headers from other projects in this solution)
3. Standard library headers (C++ standard library includes)
4. System headers (OS-specific and third-party library headers)

Separate each group with an empty line. Within each group, sort includes alphabetically. Use consistent include style (prefer #include "local.h" for local headers, #include <library.h> for external headers).

## Clang-Format
After every code change, run `make clang-format-fix` to check and fix clang-format issues.

## Single Responsibility Principle
Apply the Single Responsibility Principle to classes and methods. Each class and method should have one reason to change.

## Class File Organization
Every class should be in its own file(s) with the same name. Each class should have its own header (.h) and implementation (.cpp) files.

## Function Design
- Functions should not have side effects - return values instead of assigning as side effects
- Make two returns without else - use early returns to avoid nesting
- Reduce nesting in functions - use early continue/return to flatten logic
- Eliminate else statements - restructure conditions to avoid else branches

## Code Comments
- Remove redundant comments - only add comments that describe complex logic, not obvious code
- Don't add comments that just tell what you're doing - comments should explain why, not what

## Code Reuse
- Generalize and move common code to libraries - extract reusable functionality to appropriate libraries

## Design Patterns
- Simplify over-engineered designs - prefer simple functions over complex interfaces when appropriate
- Use maps instead of vectors of pairs - choose appropriate data structures for efficiency
- Provide arguments directly to functions - avoid unnecessary object creation when simple function calls suffice

## Variable Naming
- Never use variable names like "dummy" or "temp" - use descriptive names like "value" instead
- Choose variable names that clearly indicate their purpose and content

## Build System
- Follow conventions from other apps - use the same patterns for Makefile and CMakeLists.txt as existing applications
- Use variables and recursive rules - follow the established build system patterns 